    <!-- Main content -->
    <main class="ubg-body prose">

      <!-- Section 01 -->
      <section id="can-americans-buy">
        <h2>01 — Can Americans buy property in Roatán?</h2>

        <div class="short-answer">
          <span class="short-answer-label">Short Answer</span>
          <p>Yes. Americans can buy property in Roatán, but they should verify title, understand the ownership structure, and use qualified local legal guidance before signing.</p>
        </div>

        <p>Roatán sits within the Bay Islands of Honduras, which carry a constitutional designation that allows foreigners to own land within 40 kilometers of the coastline — a key distinction from mainland Honduras, where coastal foreign ownership is restricted. Americans can own freehold property in Roatán with the same rights as Honduran citizens, making the Bay Islands one of the most accessible Caribbean markets for U.S. buyers to enter.</p>

        <h3>Ownership structures</h3>
        <p>Property can be held in three main structures, each with different tax, succession, and exit implications:</p>
        <div class="structure-cards">
          <div class="structure-card">
            <p class="structure-title">Personal name</p>
            <p class="structure-body">Simplest structure. Direct ownership in your individual name. Common for primary residences and straightforward investment properties. Cap of 3,000 sqm (0.74 acres).</p>
          </div>
          <div class="structure-card">
            <p class="structure-title">Honduran S.A. de C.V. (corporation)</p>
            <p class="structure-body">A Honduran corporation holds the property. Useful for multiple shareholders, succession planning, and holdings over the individual cap.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="structure-card">
            <p class="structure-title">Trust (Fideicomiso)</p>
            <p class="structure-body">The property is held in trust by a Honduran bank. Common for complex estate planning or multi-owner scenarios.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="warn-box">
          <span class="warn-icon">⚠</span>
          <span>Get the structure right at entry. Changing ownership structure after closing typically requires a new deed and additional transfer taxes — it is not a free correction.</span>
        </div>

        <div class="inline-cta">
          <p class="inline-cta-heading type-chic">Not sure which structure fits your situation?</p>
          <a href="https://savvycal.com/Tomas-Figueroa-24db422b/chat-with-tomas?d=30" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="inline-cta-btn">Schedule a 30-Minute Call →</a>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- Section 02 -->
      <section id="where-to-compare">
        <h2>02 — What areas should U.S. buyers compare first?</h2>

        <div class="short-answer">
          <span class="short-answer-label">Short Answer</span>
          <p>West Bay for beach lifestyle and rental income, West End for walkable village life and diving, Sandy Bay for quiet hillside living, Pristine Bay for gated resort amenities, French Harbour &amp; East for value and long-horizon appreciation.</p>
        </div>

        <p>Roatán is not one market. The island spans roughly 40 miles from end to end, and the five main lifestyle nodes each attract a distinct buyer profile, product type, and price range. Matching the area to your goals before you start looking saves weeks of searching.</p>

        <div class="area-table-wrap">
          <table class="area-table">
            <thead>
              <tr><th>Area</th><th>Best For</th><th>Price Range</th><th>Typical Product</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td><strong>West Bay</strong></td><td>Beach lifestyle, strongest vacation rental logic</td><td>$430–$650/sqft premium oceanfront</td><td>Beach condos, resort residences</td></tr>
              <tr><td><strong>West End</strong></td><td>Walkable village, diving culture, expat community</td><td>$350–$500/sqft</td><td>Condos, small villas, mixed-use</td></tr>
              <tr><td><strong>Sandy Bay</strong></td><td>Quiet hillside, reef access, panoramic views</td><td>$300–$500/sqft (varies with elevation)</td><td>Hillside homes, view condos</td></tr>
              <tr><td><strong>Pristine Bay</strong></td><td>Gated resort amenities, turnkey management</td><td>$400–$700/sqft</td><td>Resort villas, marina residences</td></tr>
              <tr><td><strong>French Harbour &amp; East</strong></td><td>Lower entry, local character, long-horizon upside</td><td>$200–$400/sqft (wide variance)</td><td>Land, homes, early-stage projects</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>

        <p>See the full <a href="/where-to-buy-in-roatan/">Where to Buy in Roatán</a> guide for detailed buyer profiles and neighborhood breakdowns, or read the individual <a href="/neighborhoods/">neighborhood profiles</a>.</p>
      </section>

      <!-- Section 03 -->
      <section id="buying-process">
        <h2>03 — What is the buying process?</h2>

        <div class="short-answer">
          <span class="short-answer-label">Short Answer</span>
          <p>Signed letter of intent → independent attorney → title search and due diligence → purchase agreement and deposit → closing → registration. Typical timeline: 30–60 days to closing, plus 30–60 days for title registration.</p>
        </div>

        <p>A Roatán purchase moves through six clear stages. Each stage looks different from a North American transaction, but the protections exist — when you have the right people in place.</p>

        <ol class="process-list">
          <li>
            <span class="process-step-title">Letter of Intent (LOI)</span>
            <p>A written offer with price, payment terms, and your target closing date. Once accepted, earnest money — typically 10% — is wired to the listing broker's escrow account, creating a binding contract.</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <span class="process-step-title">Independent legal representation</span>
            <p>You hire your own Honduran attorney — not the developer's or the seller's recommended contact. Your attorney runs the title search, advises on structure, and drafts or reviews all documents from this point forward.</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <span class="process-step-title">Title search and due diligence</span>
            <p>Your attorney verifies ownership history at the Public Property Registry, confirms boundaries against the survey, checks for liens or encumbrances, verifies tax payments, and reviews permits. Budget 30–45 days.</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <span class="process-step-title">Promesa de Compraventa (purchase agreement)</span>
            <p>The formal purchase contract, executed by both parties. Your attorney drafts or negotiates this document before you sign. It specifies all terms, contingencies, and the closing date.</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <span class="process-step-title">Closing — Escritura Pública (notarial deed)</span>
            <p>The final deed is executed before a Honduran notary (Notario). You must be present or grant a registered Power of Attorney. Final funds wire at least 5 business days before closing. You take possession at closing.</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <span class="process-step-title">Property registration</span>
            <p>Your attorney registers the deed at the Public Property Registry. This takes 30–60 additional days. You own the property from closing — registration is the administrative step that follows.</p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </section>

      <!-- Section 04 -->
      <section id="legal-due-diligence">
        <h2>04 — What legal due diligence matters most?</h2>

        <div class="short-answer">
          <span class="short-answer-label">Short Answer</span>
          <p>Title search, boundary survey confirmation, tax and permit verification, and (for corporation purchases) corporate document review. Use an independent attorney — not one recommended by the seller or developer.</p>
        </div>

        <p>The most common buyer mistakes in Roatán are not exotic — they are due diligence shortcuts. Here are the items that matter:</p>

        <ul class="checklist">
          <li>Title search at the Public Property Registry — review the ownership chain at least 20 years prior, verify the seller's right to sell, and confirm no liens or encumbrances exist.</li>
          <li>Boundary survey — the seller provides an updated survey (less than six months old). Your attorney confirms boundaries match the legal description.</li>
          <li>Tax receipts and permits — verify all property taxes are paid and permits are in order. Outstanding taxes transfer with the property.</li>
          <li>HOA rules and rental permissions — confirm whether short-term rentals are permitted, and review HOA financial health and restrictions.</li>
          <li>Building permits for existing structures — unpermitted improvements are a title risk that does not always appear in the registry search.</li>
          <li>Corporate document review (if buying a corporation) — review the corporation's tax obligations, ownership records, and any courthouse filings.</li>
          <li>Physical inspection — hire a licensed inspector. This is not optional for resale properties.</li>
        </ul>

        <div class="warn-box">
          <span class="warn-icon">⚠</span>
          <span>Do not use the developer's or seller's recommended attorney. Conflicts of interest are common and the attorney's loyalty will be to the client paying them — which in that scenario is not you.</span>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- Section 05 -->
      <section id="how-rentals-work">
        <h2>05 — How do vacation rentals work in Roatán?</h2>

        <div class="short-answer">
          <span class="short-answer-label">Short Answer</span>
          <p>Short-term vacation rentals are the dominant income model. West Bay Beach achieves 55–75% occupancy at $150–$350 per night. Underwrite conservatively with 50–55% annual occupancy and 25–30% management cost.</p>
        </div>

        <p>Roatán's vacation rental market is driven by U.S. and Canadian visitors who come primarily for diving, beaches, and Caribbean lifestyle. The market is seasonal — high season runs November through April, with summer peaks in June and July. The off-peak shoulder months (May, September, October) require active marketing to maintain acceptable occupancy.</p>

        <h3>Rental income model — West Bay Beach benchmark</h3>
        <ul class="checklist">
          <li>Occupancy (conservative underwrite): 50–55% annually</li>
          <li>Nightly rate: $150–$350 depending on unit size, quality, and proximity to beach</li>
          <li>Property management fee: 20–35% of gross revenue</li>
          <li>Additional operating costs: cleaning between guests, HOA dues, maintenance reserve, utilities</li>
          <li>Net yield (well-located, well-managed West Bay unit): 5–8% annually</li>
        </ul>

        <p>Rental performance is highly property-specific. Units with ocean views, direct beach access, or resort amenity access outperform inland or pool-only properties by a meaningful margin. Ask for actual performance data — not projections — before buying a rental property.</p>

        <div class="warn-box">
          <span class="warn-icon">⚠</span>
          <span>Developer rental income projections are almost always optimistic. Ask for actual comparable performance data from a property manager with inventory in the same building or complex — not just the developer's own estimates.</span>
        </div>

        <div class="inline-cta">
          <p class="inline-cta-heading type-chic">Want to model the rental income potential for a specific property?</p>
          <a href="https://savvycal.com/Tomas-Figueroa-24db422b/chat-with-tomas?d=30" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="inline-cta-btn">Schedule a Strategy Call →</a>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- Section 06 -->
      <section id="pre-construction">
        <h2>06 — What should I know before buying pre-construction?</h2>

        <div class="short-answer">
          <span class="short-answer-label">Short Answer</span>
          <p>Pre-construction offers entry-price upside but carries execution risk. Verify the developer has completed prior projects, confirm escrow, review specs carefully, and budget 6–18 months beyond the stated delivery timeline.</p>
        </div>

        <p>Pre-construction is a meaningful part of the Roatán market — several active projects offer pricing 15–30% below the projected finished value. The upside is real. So is the risk. Here is what to verify before committing:</p>

        <ul class="checklist">
          <li>Developer track record — has the developer completed at least one comparable prior project on Roatán? If not, who is the construction partner, and what is their record?</li>
          <li>Escrow — are deposits held in a third-party escrow account, or paid directly to the developer? Escrow is not universal in Roatán; insist on it.</li>
          <li>Contract specifications — what exactly are you buying? Materials, finishes, square footage, amenities, and delivery timeline should be defined in writing, not left to a rendering.</li>
          <li>Title and permits — is the land fully titled in the developer's name? Are building permits issued, or pending? Pre-construction contracts on land without clear title or permits carry elevated risk.</li>
          <li>Delivery timeline — add 6–18 months to the published delivery date as a conservative baseline. Projects that finish on time exist; they are not the norm.</li>
          <li>Resale restrictions — some developers restrict resales during construction or for a defined period after delivery. Review these clauses before signing.</li>
        </ul>

        <p>See the <a href="/new-developments/">New Developments</a> page for currently active pre-construction projects with project evaluation notes.</p>

        <div class="inline-cta">
          <p class="inline-cta-heading type-chic">Looking at a specific pre-construction project?</p>
          <a href="/contact/" class="inline-cta-btn">Contact Tomas →</a>
          <a href="https://savvycal.com/Tomas-Figueroa-24db422b/chat-with-tomas?d=30" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="inline-cta-secondary">Or schedule a call →</a>
        </div>
      </section>

    </main>
  </div>

  <!-- Final CTA -->
  <section class="ubg-cta">
    <div class="container" style="max-width:720px; text-align:center">
      <Eyebrow text="Next Step" />
      <h2 class="cta-heading type-chic">Ready to talk through your situation specifically?</h2>
